# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
spinoza-ethics-3.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1 has "kinds_numerous" and "kinds_numerous → cannot_explain"; spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.2 and spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.3 have "kinds_numerous"; they differ when only "the kinds of emotions are as numerous as the kinds of objects" is true, which the second allows
spinoza-ethics-3.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the kinds of emotions are as numerous as the kinds of objects" is true and "the remaining kinds of emotions cannot be explained here" is false. Is S5, "However, I cannot here explain the remaining kinds of emotions (seeing that they are as numerous as the kinds of objects), nor, if I could, would it be necessary.", true then?
spinoza-ethics-3.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1 has "general_def_sufficient"; spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.2 and spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
spinoza-ethics-3.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1, to: Suppose "a general definition of each emotion, and understanding the general properties of the emotions and the mind, is sufficient to determine the strength of the emotions and the mind's power over them" is false. Is S7, "It is sufficient, I repeat, to understand the general properties of the emotions and the mind, to enable us to determine the quality and extent of the mind's power in moderating and checking the emotions.", true then?
spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1 has "opposed_to_luxury ∧ temperance_not_emotion → no_contraries" and "general_def_sufficient → no_need_differences"; spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.2 has "immoderate_love → species_of_desire" and "opposed_to_luxury ∧ temperance_not_emotion → no_contraries" and "kinds_numerous → cannot_explain" and "general_def_sufficient → no_need_differences"; they differ when only "by luxury, drunkenness, lust, avarice, ambition we simply mean the immoderate love of feasting, drinking, venery, riches, and fame", "luxury, drunkenness, lust, avarice, and ambition are merely species of love or desire", "temperance, sobriety, and chastity are not emotions or passive states, but indicate a power of the mind which moderates those emotions", "temperance, sobriety, and chastity are what we are wont to oppose to luxury, drunkenness, and lust", "these emotions, in so far as distinguished merely by their objects, have no contraries", "the kinds of emotions are as numerous as the kinds of objects", "a general definition of each emotion, and understanding the general properties of the emotions and the mind, is sufficient to determine the strength of the emotions and the mind's power over them" and "there is no need to take cognizance of such differences or to track out further the nature and origin of the emotions" are true, which the first allows; spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.3 has "opposed_to_luxury ∧ temperance_not_emotion → no_contraries" and "kinds_numerous → cannot_explain" and "general_def_sufficient → no_need_differences"
spinoza-ethics-3.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1 and spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.2 have "no_need_differences"; spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.3 has "great_difference ∧ no_need_differences"; they differ when only "there is no need to take cognizance of such differences or to track out further the nature and origin of the emotions" is true, which the first allows
spinoza-ethics-3.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "there is no need to take cognizance of such differences or to track out further the nature and origin of the emotions" is true and "there is a great difference between various emotions of love, hatred, or desire" is false. Is the conclusion, "Thus, though there is a great difference between various emotions of love, hatred, or desire, for instance between love felt towards children, and love felt towards a wife, there is no need for us to take cognizance of such differences, or to track out further the nature and origin of the emotions.", true then?
spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.2 takes for granted "if by luxury, drunkenness, lust, avarice, ambition we simply mean the immoderate love of feasting, drinking, venery, riches, and fame, then luxury, drunkenness, lust, avarice, and ambition are merely species of love or desire"; spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1 and spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.3 do not
spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps spinoza-ethics-3.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if by luxury, drunkenness, lust, avarice, ambition we simply mean the immoderate love of feasting, drinking, venery, riches, and fame, then luxury, drunkenness, lust, avarice, and ambition are merely species of love or desire"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
